\DOC X86_ACCSIM_TAC

\TYPE {X86_ACCSIM_TAC : thm * (thm option array) -> int list -> int list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Symbolically executes a block of x86 instructions through an {ensures} goal,
recording per-instruction accumulator equations for selected steps and leaving
the residual arithmetic obligation on the final state.

\DESCRIBE
{X86_ACCSIM_TAC execth anums snums} takes {execth : thm * (thm option array)},
the program's execution data (a length theorem for the byte list of the code
together with the per-instruction decoding array), used to step through the
loaded code; {anums : int list}, the step numbers to be summarised as
accumulated arithmetic equations (multiply/add/subtract-with-carry relations
phrased with fresh {sum_}, {carry_}, {mullo_}, {mulhi_} variables); and
{snums : int list}, the full range of step numbers to execute symbolically.
Applied to a goal whose conclusion is an {ensures} statement with precondition,
postcondition and frame describing a code fragment, it discharges the {ensures}
statement itself, leaving as conclusion the leftover mathematical condition {t}
that relates the postcondition's quantities to the newly introduced
symbolic-state variables. The assumptions {x1 ... xn} added are: the conjuncts
of the precondition, one accumulator equation per step named in {anums}, a
collection of {read}/{bytes_loaded} facts about the final symbolic state, and a
{MAYCHANGE} frame fact linking the initial and final states; occasionally an
existing assumption is deleted and re-added, i.e. moved to the end.
{
          A ?- ensures ...
   ====================================   X86_ACCSIM_TAC execth anums snums
       A, x1 ... xn ?- t
}
\noindent Alternatively no goals remain, when the residual condition is solved
outright.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only these two outcomes, on goals whose conclusion is
always an {ensures} triple for x86 code.

\ENDDOC
